.TH CCOL_SCOPED_PTR_RELEASE 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_scoped_ptr_release \- release the ownership of a scoped pointer so that it is not freed automatically
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

released = ccol_scoped_ptr_release(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the current value of name and sets name to NULL.
ccol_scoped_ptr() or ccol_scoped_ptr_mp() registered a cleanup for name.
That cleanup then becomes a no-op at the end of the scope. Use this
macro to give the ownership of the pointer to code outside the enclosing
scope, for example as the return value of a function. The pointer is
then not freed in that scope.

The caller that receives the released value becomes responsible for the
free of that value. It must free the value with the allocator that
allocated it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  A pointer previously declared with ccol_scoped_ptr() or
      ccol_scoped_ptr_mp()
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the value of name from immediately before the release (NULL if
name never received a value).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Needs GCC or Clang (ccol_scoped_ptr() and ccol_scoped_ptr_mp() already
have the same requirement)

A call on a scoped pointer that never received a value is safe. The
macro returns NULL and leaves name at NULL.

More than one call on the same name is safe. A call after the first one
has no use, because name is already NULL. Each later call returns NULL
again.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdlib.h>

char *build(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp) {
  ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(buf, char, mp);
  buf = mp ? mp->malloc(128) : malloc(128);
  if (!buf) return NULL;

  /* ... populate buf ... */

  /* after the return, the caller owns buf. The caller frees it with
     mp->free, or with free() when mp is NULL */
  return ccol_scoped_ptr_release(buf);
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr (3),
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_mp (3)
